ests across all three reviewer packages are exactly the right structural defense.\n- **One residual silent-failure risk**: if a future `matchReviewSessionState` filter is added without updating `explainEmptyManifest`, the diagnostic will hit the \"please report this as a bug\" fallback instead of explaining the real cause. Consider a sentinel/enum approach (see `/simplify` #2).\n\n### `comment-analyzer` lens\n- The docstring on `explainEmptyManifest` (lines 93-105) accurately states behavior, invariants (\"Filter precedence in this function matches matchReviewSessionState\"), and intent (distinguish handshake-failure from post-tag rejections). Good.\n- Comment at lines 124-127 (\"we just need one representative cause\") is slightly misleading in multi-agent cases — see `/review` P3 #2.\n- The lock-test docstrings (`adoptReviewEnv compares ENTIRE_REVIEW_AGENT against string(ag.Name()); drift here silently breaks review-session tagging`) — clear, gives the future reader the *why*, references the failure mode. Exemplary.\n- The inline comment in `reviewer.go:39-44` (\"A hardcoded `\"gemini-cli\"` here silently skipped adoption for every gemini review.\") is great — explains the bug the change fixed, in-place. Future readers won't need to dig through git blame.\n\n### `pr-test-analyzer` lens\n- **Coverage is strong**: one test per rejection branch, plus an integration-style `TestWritePostReviewManifest_WarnsWhenNoMatchingSessions` that updates its expected substring (`\"env-var handshake did not reach the hook\"` → `\"no session states found\"`) to match the new diagnostic.\n- **Gap**: no test exercises the \"matcher rejected all of them\" fallback. Acceptable since it's defensively unreachable, but if you add the sentinel/enum refactor, plug this gap.\n- **Gap**: no multi-agent test where `summary.AgentRuns` has 2+ entries with mixed states. Worth one test where state's `AgentType` matches agent 2 but not agent 1, to lock in the \"first agent in AgentRuns wins\" behavi